WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.280
Despite popular belief, there are actually&nbsp;
a handful of characters that David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.000
managed to do justice in the later&nbsp;
seasons. Which is where I’m going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.120
be focusing primarily because this list would&nbsp;
be full of amazing characters who died in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.120
earlier seasons like Tywin and Joffrey&nbsp;
that David and Dan physically couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:22.160
ruin. So the requirement is that they have&nbsp;
to be alive in at least season 7 or 8. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.720
I feel like it would kind of be cheating&nbsp;
if I picked minor side characters like Pod&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.640
the Rod because they have very little screen&nbsp;
time and development. Without further ado -

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.080
Number 10, Sansa Stark. Now I know this may&nbsp;
be initially controversial, but keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:42.600
it’s really hard to find 10 good characters that&nbsp;
weren’t ruined. So the first half of this list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.360
still has characters that are partially ruined.&nbsp;
Sansa out of all the main characters to me has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.880
the best executed arc that doesn’t have a bunch&nbsp;
of major flaws. There are definitely aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:56.640
about it that are still flawed like there&nbsp;
not being a lot of defining moments in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.560
arc that show her progress, and then multiple&nbsp;
minor issues that start to add up. Examples of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.320
those issues include her general stupidity in&nbsp;
certain areas like not telling Jon about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:11.000
Vale and overly trusting Littlefinger in season&nbsp;
7, and the general contriveness surrounding her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.480
character like unjustifiably hating Daenerys&nbsp;
so much. Also, it would definitely be nice if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.080
we saw more development of Sansa overcoming&nbsp;
the conflicts of running a massive kingdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.120
and the North as a whole. There’s easily a&nbsp;
lot of conflict that you can brew up in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.360
setting especially given the circumstances&nbsp;
of the story. Throughout the later seasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.800
we are essentially just told how great of a leader&nbsp;
Sansa is, but David and Dan never really show us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.240
that. They focus a lot of the show’s time in&nbsp;
other areas and probably think the complexities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.920
of running a kingdom is boring when it isn’t. Like&nbsp;
look at the earlier seasons of the show or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:49.920
House of the Dragon. Sansa essentially falls&nbsp;
victim to the rushing of the story, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:54.800
from a story perspective there’s nothing that&nbsp;
obliterates her character. It’s all partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:00.160
logical, she has a believable character arc, and&nbsp;
she has arguably the best ending out of the main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:05.120
characters. On top of her consistently being&nbsp;
relevant within the story, unlike some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:10.400
characters. So overall, you kind of have to squint&nbsp;
your eyes when viewing Sansa because her general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:16.280
form is intact, but the second you focus your eyes&nbsp;
and look at the details it mostly falls apart.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:23.040
Number 9, Samwell Tarly. Samwell as a character&nbsp;
isn’t entirely unscathed and still has some shaky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:28.680
aspects about himself. But in an overall sense,&nbsp;
Samwell Tarly from a personality perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:34.280
is relatively fine and not overly destroyed. The&nbsp;
couple bad aspects about him that I’m referring to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.800
is Samwell becoming Sam the Slayer and him easily&nbsp;
becoming the grandmaester of Kingslanding without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.880
proper training. The first one is obviously pretty&nbsp;
rough, however, I’m willing to slightly look past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:51.000
this since everyone became immortal gods with&nbsp;
plot armor during that episode. Then for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.000
second one, him becoming grandmaester is kind of&nbsp;
a fan service ending that doesn’t make sense due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.880
to his relative inexperience, but it at least is&nbsp;
the correct direction for the character. Samwell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:06.080
also doesn’t have a lot to do in the final season&nbsp;
and basically is just there to tell Jon he’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.160
Targaryen, but his season 6 and 7 goal was pretty&nbsp;
important in that he learns about the deposit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:16.600
dragon glass in Dragonstone. Along with him being&nbsp;
a P.O.V. character. His relationship with Gilly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:21.280
and his son is largely overlooked and Sam is&nbsp;
essentially the middle road character where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:26.520
David and Dan didn’t do anything great with&nbsp;
him, nor did they decimate him like Tyrion.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.840
Number 8, Tormund. Now Tormund in&nbsp;
comparison to Samwell is kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.760
the opposite. I think his character in&nbsp;
terms of how he’s implemented into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.880
story doesn’t do anything overly stupid&nbsp;
that does a disservice to him. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:46.040
I think David and Dan have severely dumbed down&nbsp;
his character since the earlier seasons. He more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:51.480
or less has become just a comedic relief character&nbsp;
when he used to also be a more intimidating, bold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:56.760
and serious wildling who has sporadic bursts&nbsp;
of comedy to release any built-up tension. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.000
makes sense for his character to become more&nbsp;
friendly over the seasons, but I think David&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.400
and Dan do it so much to the point that he loses&nbsp;
an integral aspect about himself. Like the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.400
Brienne bait romance was funny at first and it&nbsp;
essentially becomes a weird fan service-esque&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.640
meme that keeps returning that simultaneously robs&nbsp;
the character of any seriousness. Along with them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.240
doing overly silly things with the character&nbsp;
like chugging giant’s milk for a century. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:25.760
still a very fun and enjoyable figure to watch,&nbsp;
but he does lose that layer of depth from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.200
earlier seasons that balanced him out. That’s&nbsp;
why I’m putting him at number 9 since he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:35.760
this one negative aspect, but he’s still largely&nbsp;
intact to the point that he’s not overly ruined.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:41.720
Number 7, Davos Seaworth. This is a character&nbsp;
that I see a lot of love for and to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:46.840
I never really loved him that much. In his&nbsp;
earlier seasons, he’s obviously great like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:52.160
in his wonderful pairing with Shireen and his&nbsp;
morally complex pairing with Stannis Baratheon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:58.520
however, after season 5, he spontaneously joins&nbsp;
Jon’s side and becomes his yes man. Like Tormund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:03.160
I think he loses a lot of his complexity and David&nbsp;
and Dan don’t do a lot with his character to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:08.640
him overly interesting. He’s my definition of&nbsp;
the most neutral character in the later seasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:13.720
An example of an easy-hanging fruit to develop&nbsp;
Davos is the death of Mathos and whether or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:18.720
he’s still mad at Tyrion for that. Instead, that&nbsp;
goes unexplored and you probably can’t think of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.520
anything personal about the character that gets&nbsp;
developed in the later seasons. He’s kind of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.560
along for the ride and fundamentally supports&nbsp;
Jon. Like that’s fine and on a technicality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.160
he isn’t ruined, but he also isn’t doing&nbsp;
anything impressive within the story.

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:39.440
Number 6, Grey Worm. Similar to Davos, Grey Worm&nbsp;
is a very simple character that you really can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:44.200
mess up that badly. Although, David and Dan&nbsp;
still mess up one aspect about him towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:49.200
the end in which he flat out doesn’t kill Jon,&nbsp;
which essentially acts as a plot contrivance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:53.960
but besides that he has a good revenge arc. His&nbsp;
romance with Missendei was really gratifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:58.800
since it’s two characters so entrenched in misery&nbsp;
for their entire lives and they finally find love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.120
that reinvigorates them. To then have that all&nbsp;
ripped away by an evil queen makes Grey Worm’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:09.920
pure rage deeply connecting. Grey Worm because of&nbsp;
this ironically has a better mad queen arc than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.640
Daenerys because it’s much more justified&nbsp;
and you understand his perspective more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:19.560
albeit it’s obviously more simple. I just wish&nbsp;
they pushed that anger-fueled side of him more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:23.640
and had him show down against Jon at the end&nbsp;
because that’s really the more realistic path to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.800
take his character. Given that he was executing&nbsp;
prisoners for breathing. Not him allowing these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.600
traitors to do whatever they want in the end&nbsp;
and all of his anger evaporating. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.040
I think Grey Worm’s ending of honoring Missendei&nbsp;
is still pretty wholesome and works well too. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:42.680
don’t tell them that there’s apparently poisonous&nbsp;
butterflies on Naath that will decimate them.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:48.640
Number 5, Olenna Tyrell. I would put Olenna a&nbsp;
bit higher, but I feel like that would kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:53.080
be cheating since she really isn’t in the&nbsp;
later seasons all that much. David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:57.520
really nailed her two scenes within season 7&nbsp;
in which she gives some great advice to Dany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:01.720
and has one of the best death scenes in the&nbsp;
show. Like it’s genuinely pretty jarring how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:06.560
good her overall death scene was in comparison&nbsp;
to so many of the other mediocre to terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.360
scenes within the season. One aspect that really&nbsp;
carries it is that it’s long awaited payoff in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:15.760
that Olenna finally gets to tell Jaime that&nbsp;
she killed Joffrey. It leads to the juiciest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:21.240
of reactions and Dianna Rigg absolutely nails&nbsp;
the scene and utterly relishes in the pain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:26.240
misery of Jaime Lannister. That smug little&nbsp;
smile she gives Jaime is such an iconic moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.480
that it easily cements itself as one of the best&nbsp;
scenes in the later seasons. Then besides this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:36.200
her talk with Daenerys feels the most genuine&nbsp;
because she’s actually giving her good advice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:42.160
unlike Tyrion. Olenna encouraging Dany to become a&nbsp;
Targaryen and not be afraid to show her true power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:46.360
was what should have happened all along and&nbsp;
it’s too bad she didn’t listen to her. Olenna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:50.560
may only have a couple of scenes within season&nbsp;
7, but they were impactful enough to land her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:56.440
onto my list because of how perfectly executed&nbsp;
they were and how great of a character she was.

00:07:56.440 --> 00:08:03.640
Number 4, Theon Grejoy. From an overall character&nbsp;
arc perspective, Theon is pretty flawless. He has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:08.240
the most consistent and tangible arc in the show&nbsp;
to the point that it would be almost impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:13.760
mess up and David and Dan don’t. They still rough&nbsp;
it up a bit towards the beginning of season 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.480
but it wasn’t terrible. David and Dan have this&nbsp;
grand set-up for Theon’s redemption in saving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.720
Yara by finally becoming a confident and fearless&nbsp;
leader. He overcomes the nightmare of Reek and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:28.920
finally takes back his true identity of being&nbsp;
a Greyjoy. However, it seems that David and Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:33.960
didn’t really carry over this effort into season&nbsp;
8 because Theon simply waltzs into Euron’s fleet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:38.880
at night, kills a couple of guards, and rescues&nbsp;
Yara with almost no conflict. It’s a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:43.280
of a bummer that there wasn’t a grand rescue&nbsp;
kind of heist that has more impactful payoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:48.360
or even overcoming Euron, someone that Theon&nbsp;
previously ran away from. Honestly thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:53.400
about it now it would make much more sense for&nbsp;
Theon to kill Euron than Jaime. Luckily though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:58.120
this was the only mistep. His reunion&nbsp;
with Sansa is really sweet and heartfelt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.640
and the irony in that he dies protecting Bran,&nbsp;
a character he was thought of for killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.960
is actually some brilliant writing. He may&nbsp;
not have a lot of screen time and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:11.680
dialogue scenes even, but these points in&nbsp;
his character arc are pretty much perfect.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:18.240
Number 3, Jorah Mormont. Ser Jorah is just barely&nbsp;
included enough in the story to not feel left out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.240 --> 00:09:22.200
and even though I’d like some more dialogue scenes&nbsp;
for him, you could essentially say that for most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.280
of the side characters in the later seasons.&nbsp;
The main reason why I’m putting him at number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:31.560
3 is that his ending was by far the best thing to&nbsp;
have happened during The Long Night. He may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.440
kind of teleported outside of Winterfell to save&nbsp;
Daenerys in that moment, but heyyy, I’ll give it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:40.760
a pass since it makes sense from a character&nbsp;
standpoint and not necessarily a contextual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.400
logistics standpoint. But at least in comparison&nbsp;
to say Theon, there isn’t a major aspect during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.880
his journey that was very underwhelming. His&nbsp;
reunion scenes were charged with so much heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:55.480
and emotion, his character pairings were all&nbsp;
very intriguing, so much to the point that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:01.080
just want more of them, and he was even regularly&nbsp;
included in the action. Ser Jorah is essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.200
my go-to for one of the most consistently good&nbsp;
side characters. Aided with the fact that his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:11.760
overall story arc isn’t that complex so David&nbsp;
and Dan couldn’t really ruin it that badly.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:17.440
Number 2, Brienne of Tarth. I think she’s one&nbsp;
of two characters that genuinely have some great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.440 --> 00:10:22.200
character development in season 8. Because that’s&nbsp;
the main thing about Theon and Jorah in that their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.200 --> 00:10:27.520
character journeys essentially just come to an end&nbsp;
without them changing a lot. Which obviously isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:32.080
a bad thing. Meanwhile, Brienne goes through&nbsp;
an entire end goal payoff for her character in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.920
which she finally becomes a knight, she finds&nbsp;
love, and achieves an ultimate goal. On top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:41.040
there still being some good buildup to Jaime and&nbsp;
Brienne's romance in which Jaime dismantles the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:45.800
mean figure she viewed him as. Just the knighting&nbsp;
scene alone would easily put Brienne within my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:51.920
top 3 and is probably the best scene in season 8.&nbsp;
The genuine and serene happiness that gushes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:56.640
both of these characters is massively cathartic&nbsp;
and it’s one of those rare victories within Game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:01.280
of Thrones. It’s also written really well in&nbsp;
having the scene slowly progress and build the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.640
excitement as the ceremony goes on. Along with&nbsp;
Ramin Djawadi elevating the wonderful emotions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:11.200
with his outstanding music. Then you can’t&nbsp;
help but feel so much joy for the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.160
as she finally finds love in her life. That’s&nbsp;
something that she’s always struggled with and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:19.760
was emotionally beat down by, so having this&nbsp;
love scene was great for the character. Even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:23.920
though Jaime leaves right away, the love&nbsp;
doesn’t disappear and even she partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:28.280
redeems Jaime’s ending with yet another great&nbsp;
scene in which she fills the white book with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:32.760
of Jaime’s good deeds. Through and through&nbsp;
a really well done job and the main reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:37.840
why she isn’t in my number one spot is that&nbsp;
she didn’t have anything to do in season 7.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:43.120
Number 1, Sandor Clegane. I feel like the&nbsp;
primary reason why David and Dan were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:47.600
to not ruin Sandor as a character is because&nbsp;
he fundamentally plays into their writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:52.040
strengths. And those strengths are generally&nbsp;
more vulgar based comedy. Given that it seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:57.240
David and Dan fall back on that kind of dialogue&nbsp;
a lot in the later seasons. Although, Sandor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.240 --> 00:12:01.880
really the only character that wasn’t hurt by&nbsp;
this. If anything, it even helped him since he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:07.720
had a lot of iconic funny moments. Sandor uses&nbsp;
humor as both a way to destroy people and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:12.360
as a defense mechanism. The only person that&nbsp;
can get past this defense is Arya given that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:16.720
she’s spent so much time with him. Like seeing&nbsp;
them together and witnessing a more mellowed out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:22.000
Hound was genuinely outstanding and I’m really sad&nbsp;
we didn’t get more of that. Like that was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.000 --> 00:12:27.440
my favorite aspects from seasons 3 and 4. Their&nbsp;
duo is infectious and having his arc coincide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:32.760
with Arya’s in that he shows her how revenge&nbsp;
consumed himself and convinces her to escape was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:37.360
honestly pretty good. It’s a perfect arc for the&nbsp;
character and even pairs well with an additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:42.640
main character. Then his ending was also great&nbsp;
and even though it was a bit fan servicey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:47.720
I can easily forgive it given that there were&nbsp;
far greater issues about the episode. Having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:52.480
both the Hound and the Mountain die to fire&nbsp;
was surprisingly poetic. Both from a character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:57.560
perspective and his relevance within the story,&nbsp;
the Hound was consistently amazing and prevalent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.560 --> 00:13:02.880
throughout. I know him coming back into the story&nbsp;
in season 6 is kind of a hot topic, but between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:08.200
all of the absurdly dumb things that happen later&nbsp;
on, it’s not a major deal. That and the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:13.240
it put the character in was actually interesting.&nbsp;
Thank god David and Dan did a great job with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:18.720
Sandor Clegane because he has always been one of&nbsp;
my favorites. Unlike what they did with Tyrion.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.000
Thank you for watching and this video&nbsp;
was a part of a double feature. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:25.640
check out The Top 10 Characters&nbsp;
Ruined by David and Dan. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:28.760
let me know in the comments what&nbsp;
your ranking would be for this video.

